WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
Marcus and Elena are reviewing protection for themselves and their children.

2
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.860
A family plan can combine a principal insured's base coverage with

3
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.920
separate spouse or child coverage through riders.

4
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.000
A common arrangement combines permanent coverage on one adult with term

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.800
coverage on eligible family members.

6
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.700
The actual contract determines amounts, eligibility, expiration, and any

7
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:29.720
conversion rights. Under a child life rider, the insured child's covered

8
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.380
death triggers the benefit.

9
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.700
The adult owner's death is a different event.

10
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.580
Listing children as beneficiaries on an adult's policy is different from

11
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.700
insuring their lives. Begin by identifying every insured person and each

12
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.394
separate benefit. In a simplified joint first-death contract,

13
00:00:49.914 --> 00:00:53.534
Marcus and Elena are both insured under one policy.

14
00:00:54.274 --> 00:00:58.414
It pays one stated death benefit when the first insured dies

15
00:00:58.414 --> 00:01:00.054
while coverage is in force.

16
00:01:00.914 --> 00:01:04.614
Assume there are no additional riders or continuation provisions.

17
00:01:05.294 --> 00:01:10.174
After that payment, this base coverage does not promise another benefit

18
00:01:10.174 --> 00:01:12.694
when the surviving insured later dies.

19
00:01:13.514 --> 00:01:17.154
This design can address a need that arises at the first

20
00:01:17.154 --> 00:01:20.434
death, such as replacing lost household income.

21
00:01:21.494 --> 00:01:25.914
It differs from two separate individual policies, each of which can

22
00:01:25.914 --> 00:01:27.254
have its own death claim.

23
00:01:28.414 --> 00:01:32.654
Count the promised benefits instead of counting only the insured names.

24
00:01:33.852 --> 00:01:38.872
Now replace that contract with a last-survivor policy, often

25
00:01:38.872 --> 00:01:42.952
called second-to-die coverage for two insured lives.

26
00:01:43.992 --> 00:01:48.092
Its base death benefit becomes payable after the second insured dies

27
00:01:48.092 --> 00:01:50.632
while the contract remains in force.

28
00:01:51.412 --> 00:01:55.132
It can address a need expected after both parents are gone,

29
00:01:55.832 --> 00:01:59.092
such as a legacy or money for estate obligations.

30
00:01:59.912 --> 00:02:04.172
It does not, by itself, replace income for the surviving spouse

31
00:02:04.172 --> 00:02:05.732
at the first death.

32
00:02:06.572 --> 00:02:11.612
Additional riders can change a real contract's benefits, so keep

33
00:02:11.612 --> 00:02:14.200
the example's assumptions explicit.

34
00:02:16.192 --> 00:02:22.192
The first-death and second-death designs answer different timing needs even

35
00:02:22.192 --> 00:02:24.392
though both name two insured people.

36
00:02:25.038 --> 00:02:28.278
Elena owns a juvenile whole life policy on her daughter.

37
00:02:28.898 --> 00:02:32.718
Elena is the owner, her daughter is the insured, and the

38
00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:35.678
named beneficiary receives the covered death benefit.

39
00:02:36.498 --> 00:02:40.358
The policy does not insure Elena's life merely because she pays.

40
00:02:41.138 --> 00:02:45.238
Ownership can later be transferred through the applicable process, but

41
00:02:45.238 --> 00:02:50.158
adulthood alone does not prove that every contract automatically transfers

42
00:02:50.158 --> 00:02:55.138
it. A student or juvenile label also does not establish the

43
00:02:55.138 --> 00:02:58.758
amount, premium schedule, or future purchase rights.

44
00:02:59.558 --> 00:03:03.338
Check the policy and any guaranteed-insurability feature.

45
00:03:04.458 --> 00:03:08.878
A payor rider, if included, has separate conditions for protecting premium

46
00:03:08.878 --> 00:03:12.918
payments; it does not change whose life the base policy insures.

47
00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:17.588
Marcus borrows money and is offered credit life insurance.

48
00:03:18.368 --> 00:03:22.628
Maryland defines it as insurance on a debtor's life connected to

49
00:03:22.628 --> 00:03:24.748
a specific loan or credit transaction.

50
00:03:25.528 --> 00:03:29.288
Individual credit life is issued to a debtor on a term

51
00:03:29.288 --> 00:03:34.028
plan. Group credit life uses a policy issued to the creditor

52
00:03:34.028 --> 00:03:37.608
covering debtors on a term plan, with the debtor receiving a

53
00:03:37.608 --> 00:03:42.268
certificate. The covered death benefit addresses the insured debt under the

54
00:03:42.268 --> 00:03:47.028
contract. It is different from a personally owned policy naming family

55
00:03:47.028 --> 00:03:49.148
members who may choose how to use proceeds.

56
00:03:50.128 --> 00:03:53.408
Credit life is not coverage for every reason a borrower might

57
00:03:53.408 --> 00:03:58.182
miss a payment. Maryland generally limits the initial credit-life amount to

58
00:03:58.182 --> 00:04:02.542
the total repayable under the debt contract, with specific statutory

59
00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:07.722
exceptions. Determine the credit-life claim as of the debtor's death and

60
00:04:07.722 --> 00:04:09.382
use the actual benefit form.

61
00:04:10.142 --> 00:04:14.302
Net-payoff coverage includes the specified earned interest and past-due

62
00:04:14.302 --> 00:04:18.162
amounts. If a payable benefit exceeds what is due the creditor,

63
00:04:18.702 --> 00:04:22.382
the excess belongs to the person entitled under the rule, not

64
00:04:22.382 --> 00:04:24.400
automatically to the lender.

65
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.182
Early termination requires the applicable refund or credit under approved

66
00:04:28.182 --> 00:04:31.002
rules and the minimum-refund provision.

67
00:04:31.722 --> 00:04:35.862
Refinancing does not justify silently stacking the old coverage and a

68
00:04:35.862 --> 00:04:39.822
new charge. Read the documents and explain the transaction.
